WEEK 4 – PREDICTIVE MODELING AND OPTIMIZATION IN LOGISTICS
Data-Driven Logistics and Fleet Performance Optimization
1. Introduction
Week 4 extends the previous logistics analytics work into predictive modeling and optimization. The objective is to predict actual trip duration from operational characteristics and demonstrate how predictive insights can support logistics scheduling and fleet resource allocation.
2. Problem Definition
The selected problem is Trip Duration Prediction. The target variable is actual_duration_hours, a continuous numerical variable, so the task is treated as a regression problem. The selected inputs are actual_distance_miles, fuel_gallons_used, average_mpg, and idle_time_hours.
Business question: Can historical trip characteristics be used to estimate how long a logistics trip will take?
3. Dataset and Features
Feature	Role	Meaning
actual_distance_miles	Input	Distance traveled
fuel_gallons_used	Input	Fuel consumed
average_mpg	Input	Fuel efficiency
idle_time_hours	Input	Time spent idling
actual_duration_hours	Target	Actual trip duration
The trips dataset contains 85,410 records. The feature selection was also informed by Week 3 findings, where distance and duration showed a 0.99 correlation and duration and fuel showed a 0.97 correlation.
4. Data Preparation
The relevant numerical columns were selected, the target was separated from the predictors, and the data was split into 80% training and 20% testing records using random_state=42.
features = ["actual_distance_miles", "fuel_gallons_used", "average_mpg", "idle_time_hours"]
target = "actual_duration_hours"
X = trips[features]
y = trips[target]
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(
    X, y, test_size=0.20, random_state=42
)
5. Model Selection
Linear Regression was selected as a transparent baseline for approximately linear relationships. Random Forest Regression was selected as a nonlinear ensemble model that can capture interactions and nonlinear patterns.
6. Model Training
linear_model = LinearRegression()
linear_model.fit(X_train, y_train)
linear_predictions = linear_model.predict(X_test)

rf_model = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=100, random_state=42, n_jobs=-1)
rf_model.fit(X_train, y_train)
rf_predictions = rf_model.predict(X_test)
7. Evaluation Metrics
MAE and RMSE measure prediction error, with lower values being better. R² measures how much variation in trip duration is explained by the model, with higher values being better.
Model	MAE (hours)	RMSE (hours)	R²
Linear Regression	1.6317	2.1631	0.9768
Random Forest	1.6983	2.2937	0.9739
8. Model Comparison and Best Model
Linear Regression performed better on every reported metric. It achieved MAE 1.6317 hours, RMSE 2.1631 hours, and R² 0.9768. Random Forest achieved MAE 1.6983 hours, RMSE 2.2937 hours, and R² 0.9739. Therefore, Linear Regression is the preferred model for this experiment.
 
9. Actual vs Predicted Analysis
 
The Actual vs Predicted visualization provides a visual check of prediction quality. A close alignment between observed and predicted values indicates that the model captures the main variation in trip duration.
10. Prediction Error Analysis
The selected Linear Regression model has an average absolute error of 1.6317 hours on the test set. Its RMSE of 2.1631 hours indicates that larger errors receive additional penalty but remain relatively controlled.
11. Feature Importance
Random Forest feature importance shows that actual_distance_miles is the dominant feature in the selected feature set, with importance 0.969833. Fuel consumption, idle time, and MPG contribute 0.018463, 0.006869, and 0.004835 respectively.
Feature	Importance
actual_distance_miles	0.969833
fuel_gallons_used	0.018463
idle_time_hours	0.006869
average_mpg	0.004835
 
12. Example Prediction
A demonstration trip with 1,500 miles of distance, 220 gallons of fuel, 6.5 MPG, and 7 hours of idle time produced a predicted trip duration of 25.73 hours using the Random Forest model.
Predicted Trip Duration: 25.73 hours
13. Optimization Strategy
The optimization stage uses predictive insights to improve scheduling and fleet allocation. A practical objective is to reduce expected trip duration, fuel consumption, idle time, and delay-related costs while maintaining delivery requirements.
A future formal optimization model could minimize transportation cost + fuel cost + delay penalty + maintenance cost subject to vehicle capacity, driver availability, route restrictions, fleet availability, and delivery-time constraints.
14. Truck Efficiency Heuristic
A simple decision-support heuristic was calculated as average MPG divided by average idle time. It is a ranking method, not a formal mathematical optimum.
Truck	Trips	Avg MPG	Avg Idle	Distance	Score
TRK00049	912	6.5175	6.8052	1,268,102	0.957736
TRK00111	929	6.4877	6.8451	1,319,779	0.947788
