TRK00057	951	6.5435	6.9178	1,377,066	0.945897
TRK00091	901	6.5102	6.8845	1,307,376	0.945642
TRK00058	898	6.4742	6.8519	1,299,089	0.944877
TRK00098	911	6.5291	6.9247	1,307,617	0.942870
TRK00073	935	6.5031	6.8995	1,338,563	0.942552
 
TRK00049 ranked first among the supplied ten records with an efficiency score of 0.957736, average MPG of 6.5175, and average idle time of 6.8052 hours.
15. Business Recommendations
•	Use the selected duration model to improve scheduling and estimate realistic trip durations.
•	Provide additional scheduling buffer for trips predicted to require longer durations.
•	Investigate high-idle operations and identify practical ways to reduce unnecessary idling.
•	Use fleet-efficiency rankings as one input when considering suitable trucks for long-distance work.
•	Combine prediction results with route, facility, maintenance, safety, capacity, and driver-availability information before making dispatch decisions.
•	Develop a formal vehicle-routing or mixed-integer optimization model in a future phase.
16. Limitations
•	Some selected variables, such as fuel consumed and actual trip distance, may not be known before departure; therefore this is an analytical demonstration rather than a production pre-dispatch model.
•	The current model predicts actual trip duration rather than directly predicting the on-time delivery flag.
•	The truck efficiency score is a heuristic and does not guarantee an optimal assignment.
•	Real-world deployment would require traffic, weather, route, delivery-window, capacity, driver, maintenance, and availability constraints.
17. Future Scope
•	Build a pre-dispatch duration or delay model using only information available before departure.
•	Predict late-delivery probability using classification methods.
•	Integrate route, customer, facility, driver, maintenance, and safety features.
•	Apply cross-validation and hyperparameter tuning to additional models.
•	Implement formal vehicle-routing optimization using OR-Tools, PuLP, or SciPy.
•	Develop a dashboard for predicted duration, delay risk, fleet efficiency, and logistics KPIs.
18. Conclusion
Week 4 successfully applied predictive modeling to a logistics trip-duration problem. Linear Regression achieved the strongest performance with MAE 1.6317 hours, RMSE 2.1631 hours, and R² 0.9768, outperforming Random Forest on all three reported metrics.
The feature analysis identified distance as the dominant signal in the selected feature set. The optimization demonstration showed how predictive results and fleet-efficiency rankings can support better scheduling and resource allocation. The next logical step is to develop a true pre-dispatch model and formal optimization framework using operational constraints.
Appendix – Main Python Libraries and Workflow
Pandas and NumPy were used for data handling; Matplotlib and Seaborn for visualization; and Scikit-learn for train/test splitting, regression, Random Forest modeling, and evaluation.
Workflow: Data Loading → Feature Selection → Train/Test Split → Model Training → Evaluation → Model Comparison → Prediction Analysis → Feature Importance → Fleet Efficiency Heuristic → Optimization Recommendations → Business Decision Support
